<article class="env-remark" id="03II">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03II">Remark <span data-tag="03II">68.4.1</span>.</a> Before we give the proof of the next lemma let us recall some facts about étale morphisms of schemes: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
An étale morphism is flat and hence generalizations lift along an étale morphism (Morphisms, Lemmas <a href="/tag/02GS" data-tag="02GS">29.37.12</a> and <a href="/tag/03HV" data-tag="03HV">29.26.9</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An étale morphism is unramified, an unramified morphism is locally quasi-finite, hence fibres are discrete (Morphisms, Lemmas <a href="/tag/02GV" data-tag="02GV">29.37.16</a>, <a href="/tag/02V5" data-tag="02V5">29.36.10</a>, and <a href="/tag/01TH" data-tag="01TH">29.21.6</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A quasi-compact étale morphism is quasi-finite and in particular has finite fibres (Morphisms, Lemmas <a href="/tag/01TJ" data-tag="01TJ">29.21.9</a> and <a href="/tag/02NH" data-tag="02NH">29.21.10</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An étale scheme over a field $k$ is a disjoint union of spectra of finite separable field extension of $k$ (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/02GL" data-tag="02GL">29.37.7</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> For a general discussion of étale morphisms, please see Étale Morphisms, Section <a href="/tag/0257" data-tag="0257">41.11</a>. </p>
</article>